* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - TEXT MARKS, AND A COMMA BETWEEN MARKS IN A RUN.
*           Owner, 27 Aug 2026.  "~!" is a text mark, and consecutive marks are
*           written with a comma between their tokens: text mark, text mark,
*           value mark is "~!,~!,~~".  A literal comma standing in that exact
*           position is written "~,", because "~~,~~" already means two value
*           marks.
*
*           HIS FIRST PROPOSAL WAS "`~" AND HE WITHDREW IT HIMSELF, on the
*           ground that it breaks the rule that every token starts with "~".
*           That is not cosmetic: it would make the BACKTICK a second
*           escape-introducing character needing its own escape, and "~`" and
*           "`~" are anagrams, so a run of marks became unreadable.  See the
*           grammar at the top.
*
*           THE DECODE STOPPED BEING change() CALLS AND BECAME A SCAN, because
*           the run separator is a token that exists only by virtue of where it
*           sits, and change() rewrites the whole string with no notion of
*           where it is.  marks.out and marks.back.
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - THE CONVERSION IS LOSSLESS, NOT REFUSABLE.  Owner,
*           27 Aug 2026, after micro refused gpl.bp/EDIT itself: "the whole
*           purpose of these editors is primarily to edit source code without
*           having to use ED... the conversion of field, value and subvalue
*           marks needs to be handled seamlessly, just like CRLF or LF".
*
*           THE OLD GUARD REFUSED ANY RECORD WHOSE TEXT COULD BE MISTAKEN FOR
*           A TOKEN, and this record - which carries the token strings as
*           constants - was the ONLY source record in the whole shipped tree
*           that it turned away.  "~" is now an escape character: a tilde is
*           written "~-" WHERE, AND ONLY WHERE, the reader could take it for a
*           token, so ordinary text keeps its tildes readable.  Proved lossless
*           before the cycle by gplbld/test-edittokens-units.py.
* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - MARKS ARE TYPEABLE: "~~" is a value mark and "~`" a
*           subvalue mark, both ways round.  Owner's idea, and the subvalue
*           token his correction of the same day - the first version converted
*           value marks only and said a record with subvalues was not safe to
*           edit here, which was true and was not a good enough answer.
*
*           THE GUARD IS A ROUND TRIP, NOT A SEARCH FOR THE TOKENS.  Converting
*           back and comparing catches the case a token search misses: a
*           literal "~" immediately before a mark, where the substitution
*           merges with it and the tilde comes back on the other side.  A
*           record that fails it is refused and ED is named.  See
*           process.record.
* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - TWO GATES, NOT ONE.  Owner's ruling: "these commands
*           will only work if the user has OS.EXECUTE privileges.  If not, they
*           should get a message that the command is not available."  The VOC
*           tier decides who has the verb; OS.USERS field 2 decides whether it
*           runs.  check.permitted, below, and it also refuses a session with
*           no terminal - an API session or a piped script.
* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - TWO VERBS, ONE PROGRAM.  Owner, 26 Aug 2026: a
*           second full-screen editor, "one called with the verb micro and the
*           other with the verb edit".  Both VOC entries are CA $EDIT; the
*           editor is chosen from field 1 of the command line.  See THE VERB
*           CHOOSES THE EDITOR below for why this is not two programs.
* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - EDIT.  The full-screen editor comes back, on the
*           owner's ruling of 26 Aug 2026, and this is gpl.bp/MICRO ported.
*           The old MICRO source is deleted in the same commit: its VOC
*           entries went on 17 Aug 2026 and it had been callerless since.
*
*           WHAT IS THE SAME AS MICRO, because the wiring was asked to stay:
*           the record is copied to $hold, an external editor is run on the
*           copy, the copy is read back, and the user is asked whether to
*           save and - for a BP record - whether to compile and catalogue.
*           A DICT record is always saved and re-compiled with CD.
*
*           WHAT IS DIFFERENT, and each of these was a defect rather than a
*           preference:
*
*           1. THE EDITOR IS FOUND, NOT ASSUMED.  MICRO ran "!micro", which
*              on a machine without micro produced a shell error and then
*              read back an unchanged record, reporting "Record is unchanged"
*              - a confident wrong answer with nothing naming the cause.  The
*              editor is located once, up front, and a missing one refuses
*              out loud with the command that installs it.
*
*           2. IT DOES NOT GO THROUGH THE SH VERB.  "!micro" is CPROC's shell
*              escape, so it carried OS.USERS field 1 - the SH field, which is
*              not the right one for a program that runs an editor.  This
*              program is $internal, so os_permitted() admits it on
*              HDR_INTERNAL (op_sh.c:157), and check.permitted below tests
*              OS.USERS field 2 - the OS.EXECUTE field - itself.  See its
*              header for why the test is here rather than left to C.
*
*           3. THE TEMPORARY RECORD IS ADDRESSED BY ITS REAL PATH.
*              "$HOLD/name" is a relative path that only resolved because the
*              process happened to be sitting in the account directory.
*              fileinfo(FL$PATH) asks SD where the file actually is.
*
*           4. IT CLEANS UP ON EVERY EXIT.  MICRO deleted the temporary
*              record only on the "<E>xit" path, so every other way out - an
*              empty record, a write error, a refusal to save - left
*              "<name>.editing" in $hold for ever.
*
*           5. NO ABSOLUTE SCREEN POSITIONS.  MICRO wrote at fixed rows up to
*              @(0,27), which is off the bottom of a 25-line terminal, and
*              its prompts overwrote each other on a short one.  Everything
*              here is written sequentially.
* END-HISTORY
*
PROGRAM EDIT

$internal
$catalog $EDIT

$include syscom keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   q            = char(39)          ;* single quote, as ELEVATE builds its own
   ds           = @ds

*  ***THE MARK GRAMMAR.  EVERY TOKEN IS "~" PLUS ONE CHARACTER, AND "~" IS THE
*  ONLY ESCAPE CHARACTER.***  See marks.out and marks.back.  One place each, so
*  the two directions cannot disagree.
*
*      ~~   value mark          ~-   a literal "~"
*      ~`   subvalue mark       ~,   a literal "," where one would be a separator
*      ~!   text mark
*
*  THE TEXT MARK IS "~!" AND NOT "`~", which was the first proposal, and the
*  owner spotted why on 27 Aug 2026: a token led by a backtick breaks the rule
*  that every token starts with "~", and a broken rule is not cosmetic here.
*  It would make the BACKTICK a second escape-introducing character, so a
*  literal backtick before a tilde would need escaping too - a whole second
*  mechanism - and "~`" and "`~" are anagrams, so a run of marks came out as
*  "~``~" and could only be read by a strict left-to-right scanner.  With "~!"
*  a backtick is an ordinary character again and needs nothing.
*
*  THE BACKTICK IS char(96) RATHER THAN TYPED, because a lone backtick inside
*  a quoted string is the kind of character that survives one editor and not
*  the next, and this record is edited by hand.
   bq           = char(96)
   vm.token     = '~~'
   sm.token     = '~' : bq
   tm.token     = '~!'
   esc.token    = '~-'
   comma.token  = '~,'

*  The character after the "~" in each token, in one string, so the scanners
*  can test membership with index() instead of five comparisons.
   token.chars  = '~' : bq : '!-,'
   mark.letters = '~' : bq : '!'
   mark.chars   = @vm : @sm : @tm

*  What a literal "~" has to be escaped in front of: anything that would make
*  the pair look like a token, plus the three marks, whose tokens begin "~".
   danger.chars = token.chars : mark.chars

   compiled.dict = @false
   files.opened  = @false
   record.saved  = @false
   temp.written  = @false
   error.text    = ''
   input.record  = ''
   temp.record   = ''
   response      = ''

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* The command line:  edit {DICT} file record
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   cmd = convert(' ', @fm, trim(@command, ' ', 'C'))

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE VERB CHOOSES THE EDITOR, AND THERE IS ONE PROGRAM RATHER THAN TWO.
*
* Owner, 26 Aug 2026: two full-screen editors, "one called with the verb micro
* and the other with the verb edit".  Both VOC entries are CA $EDIT and this
* reads field 1 of the command line - the verb as it was typed - to decide
* which executable to run.
*
* WHY NOT TWO PROGRAMS.  Everything either verb does is the same: the working
* copy, the save query, the compile-and-catalogue loop, the cleanup.  A second
* copy of that is a second place for the next defect to be fixed in only one -
* which is the shape of MICRO's own three faults, and of the 32-bit client's
* middle hop before it was removed.  If the two ever need to behave
* differently, the table below is where it goes.
*
* THE EXECUTABLE IS NAMED, NOT PATHED.  System32 is always on PATH and a
* machine-scope winget install puts its link directory there too, so
* find.editor's lookup answers for either.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   begin case
      case upcase(trim(cmd<1>, ' ', 'B')) = 'MICRO'
         editor.exe  = 'micro.exe'
         editor.name = 'micro'
         editor.get  = 'winget install -e --id zyedidia.micro --scope machine'
*        -backup off, BECAUSE micro's DEFAULT AUTO-BACKUP CANNOT WRITE.  micro
*        2.0.15 ships "backup" true and "backupdir" empty, and an empty
*        backupdir sends the backup to MICRO_CONFIG_HOME/backups - which is
*        under C:\Program Files\SD\micro, BUILTIN\Users:(RX).  micro MkdirAll's
*        that directory on the first save; unelevated it fails and micro aborts
*        the save with "Permission denied.  Save with sudo not supported on
*        Windows", so an ordinary account cannot save at all.  PRE_RELEASE 29.
*
*        THE SAFETY NET IS THIN HERE ANYWAY: this program prompts "Save?" per
*        record, keeps the $hold working copy until every exit, and the mark
*        round trip is byte-checked before the editor is handed anything.  A
*        writable config home is the larger fix and buys nothing over those.
         editor.args = ' -backup off'
*        MICRO HIGHLIGHTS SD BASIC, AND THIS IS HOW IT FINDS THE RULES.
*        micro reads its configuration from $MICRO_CONFIG_HOME, then
*        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/micro, then ~/.config/micro (micro's options.md).
*        Only the first is any use here: the other two are per-profile, and
*        accounts SD creates cannot log in to Windows, so a syntax file in a
*        profile is one they could never be given.  SD ships its own
*        configuration directory instead and names it for the child.
*
*        kernel(K$WINPATH) because the directory is "/micro" to SD and
*        "C:\Program Files\SD\micro" to PowerShell, which cannot open the
*        first - the same conversion ELEVATE makes for its helper.
         editor.cfg  = kernel(K$WINPATH, '/micro')
      case 1
         editor.exe  = 'edit.exe'
         editor.name = 'Microsoft Edit'
         editor.get  = 'winget install -e --id Microsoft.Edit --scope machine'
*        Microsoft Edit has no syntax highlighting to configure, and it takes
*        none of micro's switches - it must be launched with the file alone.
         editor.cfg  = ''
         editor.args = ''
   end case

   if upcase(trim(cmd<2>, ' ', 'B')) = 'DICT' then
      in.file.name   = cmd<3>
      in.record.name = cmd<4>
      dict.text      = 'DICT'
   end else
      in.file.name   = cmd<2>
      in.record.name = cmd<3>
      dict.text      = ''
   end

*  The verb as typed, lower cased, so every message this program prints names
*  the command the user actually used rather than one of the two.
   verb.text = downcase(trim(cmd<1>, ' ', 'B'))
   usage     = 'Usage: ' : verb.text : ' {dict} <file> <record>'

   if trim(in.file.name) = '' then
      error.text = 'No file name specified.  ' : usage
      goto end.program
   end

   if trim(in.record.name) = '' then
      error.text = 'No record name specified.  ' : usage
      goto end.program
   end

   temp.file.name = '$hold'
   if dict.text = 'DICT' then
      temp.record.name = 'dict.' : in.record.name : '.editing'
   end else
      temp.record.name = in.record.name : '.editing'
   end

*  ***AND A BP RECORD GETS A ".sdbasic" ON THE END, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE
*  HIGHLIGHTING WORK.***  micro chooses a syntax file by matching a regex
*  against the FILE NAME, and the name it sees is this working copy's, not the
*  record's - a record called MY.REPORT tells it nothing.  The suffix is the
*  only thing that can carry the language across, and microcfg/syntax/
*  sdbasic.yaml detects exactly it.
*
*  ".editing" IS KEPT IN FRONT OF IT so a working copy left behind by a
*  session that died is still recognisable as one, whichever kind it is.
*
*  Same test as the compile offer below, and deliberately so: what is worth
*  compiling as BASIC is what is worth highlighting as BASIC.
   if dict.text # 'DICT' and upcase(in.file.name[-2, 2]) = 'BP' then
      temp.record.name := '.sdbasic'
   end

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Both gates BEFORE anything is written, so a session that may not do this is
* told so rather than handed a working copy it cannot edit.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   gosub check.permitted
   if not(permitted) then goto end.program

   gosub find.editor
   if editor.path = '' then goto end.program

   gosub open.files
   if not(files.opened) then goto end.program

   crt @(-1) :

   loop
      gosub process.record
      if trim(temp.record, ' ', 'B') = '' then
*        NAME THE FILE THE EDITOR WAS GIVEN, because this message is what a
*        failed LAUNCH looks like as well as a genuinely empty record, and on
*        26 Aug 2026 it was the second - the path was POSIX and Windows put
*        the user's typing somewhere else entirely.  Told only "Record is
*        empty" there is nothing to go on; told the path, a wrong one is
*        obvious at a glance.
         error.text  = 'Record is empty - not saved.' : char(10)
         error.text := 'The editor was given:' : char(10)
         error.text := '   ' : os.file : char(10)
         error.text := 'If you typed something and it is not here, check that'
         error.text := ' path exists.'
         goto end.program
      end

      crt
      if temp.record = input.record then
         crt 'Record is unchanged'
         record.saved = @true
      end else
         gosub save.record.query
      end

      if record.saved then
         gosub compile.record.query
         response = ''
         crt
         loop
            crt '<E>xit or <R>e-edit : ' :
            input response, 1 :
            crt
            response = upcase(response)
         until index('ER', response, 1) > 0 repeat
         if response = 'E' then goto end.program
      end
   repeat

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
end.program:
   gosub remove.temp.record

   if error.text = '' then
      crt verb.text : ' ended normally'
   end else
*     ***ONE crt PER LINE, NOT ONE crt OF A STRING WITH char(10) IN IT.***
*     Every message in this program is built with char(10) as the line
*     separator, and a bare LF moves DOWN a line without returning to column
*     0, so a single crt of the whole thing staircases across the screen -
*     confirmed on a real console by the owner, 26 and 27 Aug 2026:
*
*         edit is not available to don.
*                                     It runs an editor outside SD, so it...
*
*     A PIPE HIDES IT, which is why it survived so long: down a pipe the LF
*     *is* the separator and the same message reads perfectly.  Fixing it here
*     rather than at the eight places that build the text means the next
*     message written cannot reintroduce it.
      err.lines = dcount(error.text, char(10))
      for err.i = 1 to err.lines
         crt field(error.text, char(10), err.i)
      next err.i
   end
   stop

* ===========================================================================
* SUBROUTINES: alphabetical
* ===========================================================================

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* compile.record.query  -  a DICT record is always re-compiled; a BP record is
* offered the compile and the catalogue, exactly as MICRO offered them.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
compile.record.query:
   if compiled.dict then
      execute 'CD ' : in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name
      return
   end

   if dict.text = 'DICT' then return
   if upcase(in.file.name[-2, 2]) # 'BP' then return

   prompt ''
   response = ''
   crt
   loop
      crt 'Compile ' : in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name : '?  <Y>es, <N>o : ' :
      input response, 1 :
      crt
      response = upcase(response)
   until index('YN', response, 1) > 0 repeat
   if response # 'Y' then return

   execute 'BASIC ' : in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name
   crt

   response = ''
   crt
   loop
      crt 'Catalog?  <L>ocal, <P>rivate, <G>lobal, <N>o : ' :
      input response, 1 :
      crt
      response = upcase(response)
   until index('LPGN', response, 1) > 0 repeat

   begin case
      case response = 'L'
         execute 'CATALOG ' : in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name : ' LOCAL'
      case response = 'P'
         execute 'CATALOG ' : in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name
      case response = 'G'
         execute 'CATALOG ' : in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name : ' GLOBAL'
      case response = 'N'
         crt in.file.name : ' ' : in.record.name : ' not catalogued'
   end case
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* check.permitted  -  THE SECOND GATE.
*
* Owner, 26 Aug 2026: *"these commands will only work if the user has
* OS.EXECUTE privileges.  If not, they should get a message that the command
* is not available."*
*
* THE VOC TIER IS THE FIRST GATE AND IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN.  A programmer
* account has the verb; running an editor is still reaching the operating
* system, and who may do that is OS.USERS field 2 - the same list and the same
* field that governs OS.EXECUTE from inside a program.
*
* WHY THE TEST IS HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO os_permitted().  This program is
* $internal, so op_sh.c:157 admits it on HDR_INTERNAL and never consults the
* list.  Dropping $internal would make C do the test, but its refusal is
* k_error(sysmsg(10054)) - an abort, mid-program, AFTER the working copy has
* been written and with wording about "the operating system shell" that names
* neither this verb nor what to ask for.  Testing here refuses before anything
* is created and says what to do about it.
*
* IT MIRRORS os_permitted() DELIBERATELY, including which way it fails: a
* missing file or a missing record means NO.  An elevated session passes on
* its own, as it does for SH, so an empty list cannot lock the machine's own
* administrator out.
*
* AND THE TERMINAL TEST IS FIRST, because it is the plainer answer.  An API
* session and a piped script have nowhere to draw a full screen, and telling
* one of those about os.users would be answering a question it did not ask.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
check.permitted:
   permitted = @false

   if kernel(K$TTY, 0) = '' then
      error.text  = verb.text : ' needs a terminal to draw on, and this session'
      error.text := ' has none.' : char(10)
      error.text := 'ed, the line editor, works anywhere.'
      return
   end

   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then
      permitted = @true
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys : ds : 'os.users' to os.users.f else
      error.text  = verb.text : ' is not available: this system has no os.users'
      error.text := ' list, so nobody but an administrator may reach the'
      error.text := ' operating system.' : char(10)
      error.text := 'ed, the line editor, needs none of this.'
      return
   end

*  THE THREE-CASE FOLD, as CPROC and LOGIN's batch gate do it: as held, then
*  lower, then upper.  The record is written by hand with ED and the login
*  name arrives in whatever case Windows reports it.
   os.rec = ''
   read os.rec from os.users.f, @logname else
      read os.rec from os.users.f, downcase(@logname) else
         read os.rec from os.users.f, upcase(@logname) else os.rec = ''
      end
   end
   close os.users.f

   if downcase(trim(os.rec<2>, ' ', 'B')) = 'yes' then
      permitted = @true
      return
   end

   error.text  = verb.text : ' is not available to ' : @logname : '.' : char(10)
   error.text := 'It runs an editor outside SD, so it needs OS.EXECUTE'
   error.text := ' permission: field 2 of your record in the SD system file'
   error.text := ' os.users, which only an administrator can change.' : char(10)
   error.text := 'ed, the line editor, needs none of this.'
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* marks.out  -  temp.record (SD) -> work.record (what the editor is given).
*
* A FIELD mark is a line break, so a text editor handles fields on its own.
* The other three are control characters an editor either draws as a stray
* glyph or drops, so each becomes a two-character token; see the grammar at
* the top of the program.
*
* ***CONSECUTIVE MARKS ARE SEPARATED BY A COMMA.***  Owner, 27 Aug 2026: a run
* of marks written token against token cannot be read.  Text mark, text mark,
* value mark is "~!,~!,~~" and not "~!~!~~".  A LITERAL COMMA STANDING IN THAT
* EXACT POSITION - after a mark and before a mark - is therefore written "~,",
* because "~~,~~" already means two value marks.  Nowhere else does a comma
* need anything: they are far too common in real text to escape on sight.
*
* A LITERAL "~" IS WRITTEN "~-" ONLY WHERE THE NEXT CHARACTER WOULD MAKE THE
* PAIR LOOK LIKE A TOKEN.  Escaping every tilde would be shorter and would
* show "~-" wherever the author wrote "~", which is the complaint this whole
* conversion exists to answer.  "a~b" is "a~b" in the editor.
*
* IT COSTS FOUR count() CALLS ON A RECORD THAT HAS NONE OF THIS, which is
* almost every BASIC source record.  The scan is per character otherwise, but
* it appends whole runs of ordinary text rather than one character at a time.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
marks.out:
   work.record = temp.record

   n.special = count(work.record, '~')
   n.special += count(work.record, @vm)
   n.special += count(work.record, @sm)
   n.special += count(work.record, @tm)
   if n.special = 0 then return

   m.s = work.record
   m.w = ''
   m.len = len(m.s)
   m.run = 1                        ;* start of the plain run not yet appended
   m.prev.mark = @false

   for m.i = 1 to m.len
      m.c  = m.s[m.i, 1]
      m.nx = m.s[m.i + 1, 1]        ;* '' at the end of the record

      m.tok = ''
      m.is.mark = @false
      begin case
         case m.c = @vm
            m.tok = vm.token
            m.is.mark = @true
         case m.c = @sm
            m.tok = sm.token
            m.is.mark = @true
         case m.c = @tm
            m.tok = tm.token
            m.is.mark = @true
         case m.c = ','
*           Only the comma that would be read back as a run separator.
            if m.prev.mark and m.nx # '' then
               if index(mark.chars, m.nx, 1) > 0 then m.tok = comma.token
            end
         case m.c = '~'
            if m.nx # '' then
               if index(danger.chars, m.nx, 1) > 0 then m.tok = esc.token
            end
      end case

      if m.tok # '' then
         m.w := m.s[m.run, m.i - m.run]
         if m.is.mark and m.prev.mark then m.w := ','
         m.w := m.tok
         m.run = m.i + 1
      end
      m.prev.mark = m.is.mark
   next m.i

   m.w := m.s[m.run, m.len - m.run + 1]
   work.record = m.w
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* marks.back  -  work.record (what came back from the editor) -> temp.record.
*
* ***IT IS A LEFT-TO-RIGHT SCAN AND NOT A SEQUENCE OF change() CALLS.***  It
* was three change() calls until 27 Aug 2026 and could not stay one: change()
* rewrites the whole string for each token, so a token that only exists
* because of where it sits - the run separator - cannot be expressed, and a
* later pass can cut a token an earlier pass created.  A scan reads each
* position once and decides there.
*
* EVERY TOKEN IS "~" PLUS ONE CHARACTER, so the scan only ever has to look at
* a tilde; every other character is itself.  A record with no tilde in it
* comes back unchanged for one count().
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
marks.back:
   temp.record = work.record
   if count(temp.record, '~') = 0 then return

   m.s = temp.record
   m.w = ''
   m.len = len(m.s)
   m.run = 1
   m.prev.mark = @false
   m.i = 1

   loop
   while m.i <= m.len
      m.c  = m.s[m.i, 1]
      m.nx = m.s[m.i + 1, 1]

      m.hit = ''
      m.drop = @false
      m.is.mark = @false
      m.step = 1

      begin case
         case m.c = '~' and m.nx # '' and index(token.chars, m.nx, 1) > 0
            m.step = 2
            begin case
               case m.nx = '~'
                  m.hit = @vm
                  m.is.mark = @true
               case m.nx = bq
                  m.hit = @sm
                  m.is.mark = @true
               case m.nx = '!'
                  m.hit = @tm
                  m.is.mark = @true
               case m.nx = '-'
                  m.hit = '~'
               case 1
                  m.hit = ','
            end case

         case m.c = ',' and m.prev.mark and m.nx = '~'
*           A comma between two mark tokens is the separator this program
*           wrote, and it is not data.  The run continues across it.
            m.after = m.s[m.i + 2, 1]
            if m.after # '' then
               if index(mark.letters, m.after, 1) > 0 then
                  m.drop = @true
                  m.is.mark = @true
               end
            end
      end case

      if m.hit # '' or m.drop then
         m.w := m.s[m.run, m.i - m.run]
         if not(m.drop) then m.w := m.hit
         m.run = m.i + m.step
         m.prev.mark = m.is.mark
      end else
         m.prev.mark = @false
      end

      m.i += m.step
   repeat

   m.w := m.s[m.run, m.len - m.run + 1]
   temp.record = m.w
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* find.editor  -  ask Windows where the editor is, once.
*
* IT RETURNS THE RESOLVED PATH AND THE PATH IS WHAT IS RUN.  Asking
* Get-Command and then running the bare name would be two lookups that could
* disagree; and printing the path is what makes a wrong editor obvious rather
* than mysterious.
*
* CommandType Application is deliberate: it cannot match a PowerShell alias or
* function, only a real executable.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
find.editor:
   editor.path = ''
   probe = '$c = Get-Command -Name ' : editor.exe
   probe := ' -CommandType Application -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue; '
   probe := 'if ($c) { $c[0].Source }'

   os.execute probe capturing probe.out

   editor.path = trim(convert(char(13) : char(10), '  ', probe.out))

   if editor.path = '' then
      error.text  = editor.name : ' (' : editor.exe : ') was not found on this'
      error.text := ' machine, so ' : verb.text : ' has nothing to edit with.'
      error.text := @fm
      error.text := 'Install it from an elevated prompt:' : @fm
      error.text := '   ' : editor.get : @fm
      error.text := 'or use ed, the line editor, which needs nothing installed.'
      error.text  = change(error.text, @fm, char(10))
   end
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* open.files  -  the record's file, and $hold for the working copy.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
open.files:
   open dict.text, in.file.name to input.file then
      open temp.file.name to temp.file then
         files.opened = @true
      end else
         error.text = 'File error: ' : temp.file.name : ' could not be opened.'
      end
   end else
      error.text = 'File error: ' : trim(dict.text : ' ' : in.file.name)
      error.text := ' could not be opened.'
   end
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* process.record  -  write the working copy, run the editor on it, read it
* back.  A record that does not exist yet is editable: the copy is simply
* empty, which is how a new program is written.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
process.record:
   read input.record from input.file, in.record.name else input.record = ''
   temp.record = input.record

   if dict.text = 'DICT' then
      temp.str = trim(field(temp.record, @fm, 1), ' ', 'B')[1, 1]
      if index('CI', upcase(temp.str), 1) > 0 then
         compiled.dict = @true
         temp.record = field(temp.record, @fm, 1, 15)
      end
   end

*  MARKS BECOME TYPEABLE TEXT ON THE WAY OUT AND COME BACK ON THE WAY IN.
*  Owner's idea, 26 Aug 2026; the subvalue token the day after, and the text
*  mark and the run separator on the 27th.  The grammar is at the top of the
*  program and the two scanners are marks.out and marks.back.
*
*  ***THE CONVERSION IS LOSSLESS, NOT REFUSABLE.***  Owner's ruling, 27 Aug
*  2026, after micro refused gpl.bp/EDIT itself: "the whole purpose of these
*  editors is primarily to edit source code... the conversion needs to be
*  handled seamlessly, just like CRLF or LF".  The version this replaces
*  converted two of the three marks and then checked the round trip, refusing
*  any record whose own text could be mistaken for a token - and EDIT, which
*  carries the token strings as constants, was the ONE source record in the
*  whole shipped tree that its own guard turned away.
   gosub marks.out

*  ***AND THE ROUND TRIP IS STILL TESTED RATHER THAN REASONED ABOUT.***  It is
*  now expected never to fire: gplbld/test-edittokens-units.py runs these two
*  algorithms against each other over EVERY string up to six characters drawn
*  from ~ ` ! - , @vm @sm @tm and one ordinary letter, and over all 197 shipped
*  gpl.bp records, and nothing is lost.  It stays because losing a user's data
*  silently is the one outcome worth a few lines to prevent, and because a
*  failure here would now be a defect in THIS program rather than a property of
*  the record.
*
*  IT COSTS A THIRD SCAN, and that is the price of the check being real: this
*  runs marks.back on the actual text the editor is about to be given rather
*  than reasoning about what it would produce.
   check.keep  = temp.record
   check.in    = work.record
   gosub marks.back
   check.back  = temp.record
   temp.record = check.keep
   work.record = check.in

   if check.back # temp.record then
      error.text  = verb.text : ' cannot represent this record safely, which is'
      error.text := ' a fault in SD rather than anything wrong with the record.'
      error.text := char(10)
      error.text := 'Nothing has been changed.  Please report it.' : char(10)
      error.text := 'ed, the line editor, handles the record meanwhile.'
      goto end.program
   end

   write work.record to temp.file, temp.record.name on error
      error.text = 'Write error: the working copy could not be created in '
      error.text := temp.file.name
      goto end.program
   end
   temp.written = @true

*  THE REAL PATH, NOT "$hold/name".  $hold is a directory file, so its record
*  is a file on disk, and fileinfo() is what knows where.  MICRO passed a
*  relative path and it resolved only because the process happened to be
*  standing in the account directory.
   hold.dir = fileinfo(temp.file, FL$PATH)
   if hold.dir = '' then
      error.text = 'Could not find the operating-system path of ' : temp.file.name
      goto end.program
   end

*  ***AND THEN CONVERTED, BECAUSE fileinfo() ANSWERS IN POSIX.***  Measured
*  26 Aug 2026 with ANALYSE.FILE on an installed system:
*
*      Path name : /cygdrive/c/ProgramData/SD/sdsys/$hold
*
*  sd.conf holds "C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys", so it is tempting to conclude the
*  runtime keeps Windows paths and skip this.  IT DOES NOT: the MSYS2 runtime
*  converts on the way in and SD works in POSIX from then on.
*
*  A POSIX PATH HANDED TO A WINDOWS PROGRAM IS NOT AN ERROR, WHICH IS WHY THIS
*  COST A TEST RUN.  "/cygdrive/c/..." reads to Windows as a path from the
*  root of the CURRENT DRIVE, so the editor neither refuses nor opens the
*  record - it opens a new buffer somewhere else entirely.  micro says
*  "parent dirs don't exist"; Microsoft Edit says nothing at all and the
*  record reads back empty, which arrives as "Record is empty - not saved".
*  A stray "C:" directory in the account was the only physical trace.
*
*  kernel(K$WINPATH) is the conversion - cygwin_conv_path(), op_kernel.c:628 -
*  and it is the same one ELEVATE makes for its helper, whose header says it
*  plainly: the scripts are POSIX "/" to SD and "C:\Program Files\SD" to
*  PowerShell, WHICH CANNOT OPEN THE FORMER.
*
*  IT ANSWERS AN EMPTY STRING RATHER THAN A GUESS when the runtime cannot
*  convert, so an empty answer is refused here rather than passed on.  A wrong
*  pathname would put the user's work in a file nobody goes looking for.
   posix.file = hold.dir : ds : temp.record.name
   os.file = kernel(K$WINPATH, posix.file)
   if os.file = '' then
      error.text  = 'Could not turn the working copy path into a Windows one:'
      error.text := char(10) : '   ' : posix.file
      goto end.program
   end

   crt @(-1) : 'Editing ' : trim(dict.text : ' ' : in.file.name) : ' ' :
   crt in.record.name

   cmd = ''
   if editor.cfg # '' then
      cmd := '$env:MICRO_CONFIG_HOME = ' : q : editor.cfg : q : '; '
   end
   cmd := '& ' : q : editor.path : q : editor.args : ' ' : q : os.file : q
   cmd := '; exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   os.execute cmd
   rc = os.error()

   crt @(-1) :

   read work.record from temp.file, temp.record.name else
      error.text = 'The working copy has gone - nothing was read back.'
      if rc # 0 then
         error.text := '  The editor exited ' : rc : '.'
      end
      goto end.program
   end

*  Back the other way, so what is compared and what is written are both in
*  SD's own representation.  This is also where anything the USER typed is
*  interpreted, which is what makes the tokens a feature rather than only a
*  transport.
   gosub marks.back
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* remove.temp.record  -  on EVERY exit, not only the tidy one.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
remove.temp.record:
   if temp.written then
      delete temp.file, temp.record.name on error
         crt 'Warning: the working copy ' : temp.record.name : ' is still in '
         crt temp.file.name
      end
      temp.written = @false
   end
return

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* save.record.query  -  a DICT record is always saved, as in MICRO.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
save.record.query:
   if dict.text = 'DICT' then
      response = 'Y'
   end else
      prompt ''
      response = ''
      crt
      loop
         crt trim(dict.text : ' ' : in.file.name) : ' ' : in.record.name :
         crt ' has been changed'
         crt 'Save?  <Y>es, <N>o : ' :
         input response, 1 :
         crt
         response = upcase(response)
      until index('YN', response, 1) > 0 repeat
   end

   if response # 'Y' then
      error.text = 'Record not saved'
      goto end.program
   end

   write temp.record to input.file, in.record.name on error
      error.text = 'Write error: ' : trim(dict.text : ' ' : in.file.name)
      error.text := ' ' : in.record.name : ' could not be updated.'
      goto end.program
   end
   record.saved = @true
return

end
